#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# complete_integral

#% text
# `complete_integral(eq, u(x1, ..., xn), [x1, ..., xn])` gives a complete integral of the first-order partial differential equation `eq`: a solution with `n` independent arbitrary constants `c(1), ..., c(n)`. With `u` alone the solution is a pure function.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Separable equations and Clairaut equations `u == x p + y q + g(p, q)` are solved.

#% item
# Equations `F(x, p) = 0` in the derivatives `p_i = d(u, x_i)` that are free of `u` and depend explicitly on at most one variable `x_k` are solved by separation: the other derivatives are constants `c(j)`, `p_k` solves the equation, and `u = integrate(p_k, x_k) + sum c(j) x_j + c(n)`.

#% item
# Other equations stay unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
complete_integral(d(u(x, y), x) - d(u(x, y), y)^2 == cos(x), u(x, y), [x, y])
#% output
#= [[u(x, y) -> c(2) + x*c(1)^2 + y*c(1) + sin(x)]]

#% cell
complete_integral(d(u(x, y, z), x)^3 == d(u(x, y, z), y) + d(u(x, y, z), z), u(x, y, z), [x, y, z])
#% output
#= [[u(x, y, z) -> c(3) + x*c(1) + y*c(2) + z*c(1)^3 - z*c(2)]]

#% cell
sol = complete_integral(d(u(x, y), x)*d(u(x, y), y) == 1, u, [x, y])

#% cell
rewrite(d(u(x, y), x)*d(u(x, y), y) - 1, sol[1])
#% output
#= 0

